<h2>A marriage of months, litigation of decades</h2>
<p>The parties married in August 2007 under circumstances that generated dispute almost immediately: the wife left the matrimonial home around January 2008, each side blaming the other. The husband\'s restitution suit was withdrawn; his divorce suit was dismissed for default; the wife\'s Section 125 application came only in 2014. In March 2018, the Family Court ordered Rs. 25,000 per month with Rs. 20,000 in litigation costs — an order the husband, asserting a salary of about Rs. 45,000 and pointing to the wife\'s qualifications and property, challenged in revision. The revision took eight years to decide, during which the husband was arrested over arrears and paid part of them.</p>

<h2>The two findings a maintenance court cannot skip</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>The claimant\'s inability</strong><p>Section 125 protects a wife "unable to maintain herself". That inability must be pleaded and proved — education, work history, income, assets and health all bear on it. The High Court faulted the record for treating entitlement as self-evident, and noted the long, unexplained delay in claiming maintenance at all.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The respondent\'s income</strong><p>Quantum must be anchored in a finding of what the husband actually earns. His failure to appear or cross-examine permits adverse inferences under Section 114(g) of the Evidence Act, but inference is not exemption: the court must still gather material — affidavits, salary records, standard-of-living evidence — and determine income before fixing a figure.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The Rajnesh v. Neha architecture</h2>
<p>Since <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> (2020), every maintenance proceeding is supposed to run on sworn affidavits of assets and liabilities from both sides, updated where circumstances change, with quantum reasoned against the disclosed material. <em>Kiran Jyot Maini v. Aneesh Pramod Patel</em> (2024) carried the framework forward with directions on enforcement. The remand order here is in effect a direction to run the proceeding the way those judgments require: fresh affidavits, evidence from both parties, and a time-bound trial without adjournments. The interim figure of Rs. 10,000 per month preserves the wife\'s subsistence while the record is built.</p>

<h2>Lessons for both sides of a maintenance case</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>If you act for the claimant</th><th>If you act for the respondent</th></tr>
<tr><td>Plead and prove inability to maintain — do not rest on the marital status alone</td><td>Appear and contest; absence invites adverse inference even though it does not excuse the court from finding income</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explain any delay in approaching the court</td><td>File a complete, honest affidavit of assets; understatement discovered later destroys credibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bring standard-of-living and income evidence of the respondent</td><td>Document actual income — salary slips, tax returns, bank statements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seek interim maintenance early with supporting affidavit</td><td>Pay interim orders promptly; arrears can lead to arrest and colour the final hearing</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The provision today</h2>
<p>From 1 July 2024, applications of this kind are filed under Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, which reproduces Section 125 CrPC with the same classes of claimants — wives, children and parents — and the same remedial purpose. Pending proceedings instituted under the old Code continue under it, which is why this revision was decided under Section 125. The substantive law the judgment applies — proof of inability, determination of income, the affidavit regime — is identical under both provisions.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The revision was allowed in part: the final order of Rs. 25,000 was set aside, but the husband\'s liability was not extinguished — the matter returns to the Magistrate for a fresh, evidence-based determination with interim maintenance of Rs. 10,000 per month running meanwhile.</p>
</div>
